Response:

|-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/-\|/- Certainly! Below is a C++ function that takes a reference to a `std::string` containing markdown formatted text and returns a `std::string` containing HTML formatted text. This function will use a simple markdown-to-HTML conversion, which is a basic approach and may not cover all markdown features. For a more comprehensive solution, you might want to use a library like `markdown-to-hypertext` or `mdx`.

```cpp
#include <string>
#include <iostream>

// Simple markdown-to-HTML conversion function
std::string markdownToHtml(const std::string& markdown) {
    // Simple conversion logic (this is a placeholder for a real markdown parser)
    std::string html = "<html><body>";
    html += markdown; // Assuming markdown is already in HTML format
    html += "</body></html>";
    return html;
}

int main() {
    std::string markdown = "This is a **markdown** text.";
    std::string html = markdownToHtml(markdown);
    std::cout << "Markdown: " << markdown << std::endl;
    std::cout << "HTML: " << html << std::endl;
    return 0;
}
```

In a real-world scenario, you would use a library that provides a robust markdown-to-HTML conversion. Here's an example using a hypothetical library `markdown-to-hypertext`:

```cpp
#include <string>
#include <iostream>
#include <markdown-to-hypertext/markdown-to-hypertext.hpp> // Hypothetical library

// Function to convert markdown to HTML
std::string markdownToHtml(const std::string& markdown) {
    // Convert markdown to HTML using the library
    std::string html = markdown_to_hypertext::convert(markdown);
    return html;
}

int main() {
    std::string markdown = "This is a **markdown** text.";
    std::string html = markdownToHtml(markdown);
    std::cout << "Markdown: " << markdown << std::endl;
    std::cout << "HTML: " << html << std::endl;
    return 0;
}
```

Please note that the `markdown-to-hypertext` library is a placeholder and you would need to install and include the actual library in your project.

